Years passed while Keep held her thread. It never broke, never weakened, but it never became anything either. She stayed cold while the thread that could have been a blanket stayed wound around her trembling hands.

One winter night, a child shivered nearby. Keep's fingers, numb from holding so long, finally loosened. The thread slipped free and fell across the child's shoulders.

The moment it touched another, the thread began to multiply. It wove itself into fabric, then into more thread, then into looms that appeared from nowhere. Soon everyone had thread, weaving tapestries that told stories, blankets that shared warmth, ropes that connected distant hearts.

Keep stood wrapped in cloth woven from her original thread, now returned to her a thousandfold.

This is why thread connects: Keep learned that what we hold too tightly stays single, while what we release weaves worlds.

